WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:20.020
Improving the Quality of Customer Insight through NHS Citizen

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:29.039
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: This paper summarises the
changes that we are proposing to NHS Citizen,

00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:34.030
and the approach has been developed over the
past few months mainly following a report

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:41.040
that was published in May, which was a learning
review about how NHS Citizen had both been

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.440
delivered, and some of the positives, and
some of the things that could be done better.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:53.800
That review was actually put together by the
four organisations that we commissioned to

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.490
deliver NHS Citizen, and had quite wide engagement
from lots of different key stakeholders, but

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:03.850
also people involved in Citizen from the very
beginning; and we know that we had around

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:09.040
4,000 citizens who were involved in the original
programme, so quite a wide spread.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:16.600
What it showed was that the Citizen had developed
into a really valued brand, so lots of people

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:22.460
welcomed it: they liked the name, they liked
what it stood for. We have something like

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:28.499
800&nbsp;patient representatives across our different
groups in NHS England at the moment, which

00:01:28.499 --> 00:01:34.189
has been really positive. However, the review
recommended that, although we should retain

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:41.950
the NHS Citizen brand, we should think slightly
differently about how we implement it in the

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:49.420
future. We could concentrate on having a much
broader and more comprehensive picture of

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:54.369
citizen views; so that we looked at even some
of the things that were often quite uncomfortable

00:01:54.369 --> 00:02:00.590
for us to hear, because that is a really important
part of engaging with our patients and the

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:04.600
public, but that we also looked at a wider
range of sources, so things like surveys as

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:09.580
well as insight, use of social media, etc.
The review also recommended that we had a

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.830
much greater focus on our business priority
areas, and some of the feedback we had had

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:19.360
from some of the Citizen Assembly events was
that, while the subject matters and the things

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.080
that people were bringing were really important
and we needed to give due attention to that,

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.290
we had agreed – both with the public and
patients, but also as a Board – what our

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.939
national priorities are, and we needed to
make sure that we were engaging more widely

00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:38.349
in those particular areas. We also needed
to make sure that we had an opportunity to

00:02:38.349 --> 00:02:44.920
work in much closer collaboration with our
other partners. Everybody has a duty to involve,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:49.349
and everybody has a duty to engage with patients
and the public, and it made a lot of sense

00:02:49.349 --> 00:02:54.950
for us to do that in partnership with other
ALBs and for organisations such as Healthwatch.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:03:02.780
The new NHS Citizen approach is deliberately
planned to be a much more comprehensive analysis,

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:07.700
and we have both patient and public intelligence
and insight, and very direct feedback, as

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:15.760
well as looking more widely. We identified
that we should have several key areas that

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.423
we will continue to build on as we move forward.
The first one which is set out in the paper

00:03:20.423 --> 00:03:24.590
– and I will not go into detail, because
you will have all read it – is Citizen Online.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:29.489
That is really a repository for a huge variety
of data, so places where people can go and

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:31.110
look and bring it all together.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:37.940
Also, an outreach programme where we could
have dedicated expertise and support to reach

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:43.750
out to people, to understand what it is they
want to say and understand the different groups

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:51.930
and populations that we seek to work alongside
and provide care for. Some good examples of

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:56.740
that are the Youth Forum that we have running,
and many of the Board will be aware of that.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:03.079
We have had Youth Forum representatives both
at NHS Expo and at our AGMs, etc., and some

00:04:03.079 --> 00:04:07.879
of the work that we have been able to do with
the Youth Forum has had a significant impact

00:04:07.879 --> 00:04:15.969
on our ability to respond to the needs and
wishes of children and young people.

00:04:15.969 --> 00:04:20.540
We also have some good examples with our work
with learning disability, and many of the

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:25.970
Board will know that we employ people within
our engagement team with a learning disability

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:33.160
who have a specific responsibility for supporting
our engagement with that group of individuals

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:38.320
that we have our massive programme of transforming
care to support.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.130
Citizen representatives, which will bring
together all of those individuals that are

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:49.300
engaged in our Forum and the different national
priorities, and we will do that through a

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:53.080
customer relationship management system, which
we are going to call People Bank, so that

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:58.440
we have a really good bank of people that
we know want to be involved.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:03.690
We have an involvement hub, which we will
develop over the coming months, which will

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:08.690
provide support and information for those
who want to be involved in providing us with

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:14.270
advice, guidance and support, but also for
our staff, to support them to be able to engage

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:16.210
in an appropriate way.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:21.340
Underpinning all of that will be a communication
plan, which we hope will allow us to really

00:05:21.349 --> 00:05:33.690
maximise the brand and to really try and simplify
how we do that. It should be and it must be

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:39.000
business as usual. This is not something that
a small group of people do; this is something

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:44.330
that needs to be almost hardwired into what
we do, into how we do it; something that everybody

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:49.750
thinks about with every decision we make.
Going back to the old, ‘No decision about

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:54.780
me without me’: think like our patients
and public, ask what matters to you, to the

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:59.410
individuals we are supporting, and look at
how we can do that differently.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:06.039
On 12th September, the Public and Patient
Engagement team held an event with about 70

00:06:06.039 --> 00:06:11.020
to 80&nbsp;people, who were existing citizens
who are involved in the NHS Citizen programme

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:17.900
but also a wider range of stakeholders. At
that meeting, these developments were discussed

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.539
and proposed, and there was some widespread
support for them.

00:06:21.539 --> 00:06:27.660
Two things were flagged, though, and they
are reflected in the paper. One was the desire

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:31.640
for citizens to continue to be involved in
the governance of the programme, and you can

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:36.260
see that reflected in the next steps, where
we have suggested that the programme board

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:43.220
that was set up to provide oversight of Citizen
includes citizens as part of that group. Also,

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:49.750
that we made sure we were able to reach a
diverse range of communities, and it was not

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:56.400
just a few people who felt they could contribute
or found it easy to contribute that we listened

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.620
to, and we really did think more widely about
the broad spectrum of support that we should

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.740
provide and the people we should listen to.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.940
The last thing I really want to say before
opening it up and seeing whether any colleagues

00:07:09.949 --> 00:07:15.809
want to contribute was that one of the pieces of work that NHS Citizen will

00:07:15.809 --> 00:07:19.529
contribute significantly in the coming months will be working alongside the STPs

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:26.520
Providing the STPs with an offer of support and actually tagerting some support where we think

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:31.640
and they think it can be best used, so actually really contributing to the STP process

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:34.440
and the angagement with patients and the pbulic in those areas,

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:42.240
so, i think we got a reallt exciting opportunity to do even better than we have done, not losing the benefits

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.780
of what went before, but building on that and turning this into something that is very meaningful

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:48.980
for the patients and population that we work with.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:56.580
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you Jane, we know the STPs have now chance of working unless there has been extensive public and patient engagement

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:01.240
so I think an offer of assistance on that front will be very warmly appreciated

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:04.720
Let me invite any comments please, from the board.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:09.900
Michelle.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:17.300
Thank you Jane.  Really helpful paper.  Two points from me.  One is, there as been some great examples

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:23.140
of co-production on some of the big national priorities, Mental Health, Learning Disabilities,

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:28.740
Cancer, Diabetes.  Would you want to draw out any key points and learnings from those,

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:34.340
which denote quite a change of attitude in the way in which NHS England is working

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.820
and secondly on Malcolm's point

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:44.660
What potential support offer could we give to STPs, or work in parellel with STPs

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:50.200
particularly looking at the NHS Citizen outreach work that you are proposing

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:56.580
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: So, I think the lessons, certainly the lessons colleagues may want to add to it,

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:01.700
but the lessons from me about the work we have done, in terms of co-production, has been sometimes

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:09.200
for individual people it's a bit daunting because some people find it can be daunting becasue you don't know

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:12.800
what we're going to be asked. There is always a fear, I think,

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:14.640
for a lot of the 
people in the NHS that we are actually not

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:22.200
going to be able to deliver what people might
ask for. A really clear change in emphasis

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:27.779
from a very simple wording: instead of saying,
‘What is the matter?’, you say, ‘What

00:09:27.779 --> 00:09:31.350
matters to you?’, actually does help quite
significantly.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:37.060
For example, at Expo, we did a panel discussion
with members of the audience and others around

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:40.149
‘what matters to you’ and the impact that
has made. So if you are looking at things

00:09:40.149 --> 00:09:45.110
like the Always events that we have talked
about recently with learning disability, actually

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:49.339
asking people what matters to you, and what
matters to you about how you are discharged

00:09:49.339 --> 00:09:54.670
from a hospital service, has actually completely
changed the way people responded, and it can

00:09:54.670 --> 00:10:00.380
be really simple things that make a real difference.
We found that actually doing proper co‑production

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:09.550
as opposed to just talking about it has made
improvements in patient experience much easier,

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:13.900
and actually much better for people, and at
the end of the day that it what we are here

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:18.050
for. We are here to improve the experience,
improve outcomes, and to make the best use

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:25.450
of the money we have. I really would encourage
more people to do that, and to sometimes take

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:29.910
a bit of a step into the unknown. Sometimes
it can be daunting, but when you do it, it

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:33.390
actually makes things a lot easier.

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:41.010
In terms of the STPs, we have produced some
support and some information and some guidance,

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:48.090
as we do. I think there is something around
some really practical support that people

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:56.279
may want to access, and we can look at what
the specific needs of the STPs and the local

00:10:56.279 --> 00:10:59.480
population are, and actually try and target
that where it can have the biggest impact,

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:03.070
and that is what we are planning to do over
the coming weeks and months.

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:09.760
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Victor, I know you are chairing
a task force which is of great importance

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:14.630
to us in carrying this programme forward.
Have you some reflections on the change in

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:19.180
direction for NHS Citizen that the Board should
be thinking about?

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:24.730
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I was thinking about Lao-Tzu,
actually, since he has been quoted already.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:29.350
Wherever we end up with this, we should go
on the journey with all our hearts, and what

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:36.050
I mean by that is the NHS Citizen programme
was designed with citizens, and it was the

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:43.079
first time that that had been done, really.
In its design, it was designed to challenge

00:11:43.079 --> 00:11:51.710
NHS England’s culture, which is uncomfortable
and difficult, and we need to be brave in

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:54.980
engaging with citizens, because they are going
to say things – and they should say things

00:11:54.980 --> 00:12:01.170
– that we will find uncomfortable. The process
of getting here has been quite challenging,

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:04.620
and I personally have put a lot of time into
this.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:17.610
If I may, Chair, I think we should thank the
over 4,000 people, because actually they put

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:27.670
a lot of effort into the design of NHS Citizen,
and I really do hope that this redesign invites

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:33.529
them to continue in a way that continues to
be challenging for us. If it does not, then

00:12:33.529 --> 00:12:40.920
I think we are failing; it is as simple as
that. Because I was not there on the 12th&nbsp;September

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:50.449
debate; I have received some communication
from people who were, about the tensions between

00:12:50.449 --> 00:13:00.410
the desires of the public, NHS citizens, to
say what they feel, and our desire to stick

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:04.209
to priorities. I understand that; that is
what happens.

00:13:04.209 --> 00:13:10.160
I welcome the relationship with Healthwatch
England. I think, as Chair of the task force

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:19.350
that you have asked me to set up, that NHS
Citizen should deliver a big chunk of the

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:28.921
value to achieving the Five Year Forward View
ambitions in chapter two. However, I think

00:13:28.921 --> 00:13:35.490
NHS England, alongside Citizens, have a lot
of learning. This is new territory, this is

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:40.980
new work, and we are going to have to develop
new skills and new insights on the way. It

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:47.150
will not always be comfortable, but as I say,
we must go with all our hearts.

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:52.279
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Very, very well put. Being
comfortable is not a position we are accustomed

00:13:52.279 --> 00:13:56.350
to on the Board.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Tell me about it!

00:13:56.350 --> 00:14:00.730
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think those comments were
very, very wise.

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:07.130
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Victor and I have talked
about this a lot. We agree. I completely understand

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:11.880
that. As I said, some of this is uncomfortable,
but that is because it is the right thing

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:15.329
to do.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Actually, it is uncomfortable

00:14:15.329 --> 00:14:19.800
because we fear it disrupts an established
status quo, as opposed to it being uncomfortable

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:22.490
because we disagree with the direction of
travel.

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:29.280
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Exactly. I think what Victor
said summarised very well what many of us

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:35.660
and many of the people involved in Citizen
think. I think this is the way forward that

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:41.709
actually enables us to do both, but we should
not forget why it was set up in the first

00:14:41.709 --> 00:14:45.760
place and what we are trying to achieve.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you. On that basis,

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:52.290
may I ask the Board to endorse the new approach
for NHS Citizen, bearing in mind the discussion

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:56.149
that we have just had and, in particular,
the contribution we have had from Victor?

00:14:56.149 --> 00:14:56.649
Okay.

